  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2006-p19.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let the angles be \\(n - d,\\) \\(n,\\) \\(n + d.\\) Their sum is \\(3n = 180,\\) so \\(n = 60.\\)\n\nThe measures are distinct positive integers, so \\(d \\ge 1,\\) and \\(n - d \\gt 0\\) forces \\(d \\lt 60.\\) Thus \\(d \\in \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 59\\},\\) giving \\(59\\) non-similar triangles.\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "If the angles are in arithmetic progression, the middle angle is \\(60^\\circ\\)",
        "The common difference \\(d\\) satisfies \\(1 \\le d \\le 59\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
